# Quink SPA — copy to .env.local and fill in. Never commit .env.local.
#
# EVERY value here is PUBLIC. Vite replaces `import.meta.env.VITE_*` with a string literal
# at BUILD time, so there is no such thing as a hidden VITE_ variable — the prefix is the
# opt-in to being in the bundle. That is expected and safe for all four: the anon key only
# grants what RLS allows, and the other three are addresses and a label.
# Never put the service role key here. It bypasses RLS and belongs only in worker/.env.
#
# VERCEL WARNS ABOUT THIS AND THE WARNING IS EXPECTED. It fires on the VITE_ prefix, not on
# the value. Do NOT "fix" it by marking these Sensitive in Vercel — that flag only stops the
# dashboard showing the value back to YOU; the build still needs it and still inlines it.
# And do NOT drop VITE_APP_ENV to infer the environment from the hostname instead: that is
# forbidden by CLAUDE.md §10m, which names the bug it caused last time.

VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://YOUR-PROJECT-REF.supabase.co
VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# The FastAPI pipeline worker. Vite inlines this at BUILD time, so it must be set in the
# DEPLOY environment (Vercel > Settings > Environment Variables) too — and changing it there
# only takes effect on the next build, not on the running deployment.
# Unset in production the bundle would fall back to http://localhost:8000 and the deployed
# site would call each visitor's own machine, so the build now fails instead (vite.config.ts).
VITE_WORKER_URL=http://localhost:8000

# WHICH DEPLOYMENT IS THIS: "production" or "staging". REQUIRED — no default. Inlined at
# BUILD time, so it is set per Vercel environment, and changing it there only takes effect
# on the next build. Anything but "production" renders the STAGING bar across the top of
# the authoring app (never on the reader — that must look identical in both environments).
VITE_APP_ENV=staging
